<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Swift Deep Research entitlements.

  The App Sandbox is intentionally DISABLED (no com.apple.security.app-sandbox
  key, empty <dict/>). This is a deliberate, required design decision — NOT an
  oversight:

  The knowledge base is backed by a pyseekdb sidecar that the app auto-launches
  so users never touch a terminal. SidecarSupervisor bootstraps this itself by:
    * spawning child processes (Foundation.Process / posix_spawn / exec),
    * creating a private Python virtualenv (`python3 -m venv`),
    * running `pip install -r requirements.txt` to fetch the sidecar's deps,
    * launching `python3 seekdb_sidecar.py` as a process-group leader, and
    * reaping the process tree (killpg) on quit.

  The App Sandbox forbids arbitrary child-process exec and writing/creating an
  executable virtualenv outside the container, so enabling it would break the
  "the knowledge base just works on first launch" flow entirely. The app is
  distributed unsandboxed for this reason; SidecarSupervisor additionally
  surfaces an actionable error if it ever finds itself running inside a sandbox
  (APP_SANDBOX_CONTAINER_ID present) and a launch fails.

  If sandboxing is ever required (e.g. Mac App Store), the sidecar must be
  redesigned to avoid spawning Python/pip — for example by bundling a
  pre-built, signed helper tool and using a sanctioned IPC mechanism.
-->
<dict/>
</plist>
